<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vincenzo Scamozzi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1548">1548</a>–<a href="/years/1616">1616</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vincenzo Scamozzi was an Italian architect from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a>, the leading successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-palladio">Andrea Palladio</a>. He completed Palladio's Teatro Olimpico in Vicenza, adding its famous perspective street scenery, designed the Procuratie Nuove on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-the-evangelist">Saint Mark</a>'s Square in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teatro-allantica">Teatro all'Antica</a> at Sabbioneta. His treatise L'idea della architettura universale (1615) influenced architects across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, including Inigo Jones.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vincenzo-scamozzi.jpg" alt="Vincenzo Scamozzi" width="320" height="365" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paolo Veronese · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scamozzi_portrait_by_Veronese.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vincenzo Scamozzi was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a> on 2 September 1548. His father, Gian Domenico Scamozzi, was a surveyor and building contractor and was his first teacher, instructing him in the principles of Sebastiano Serlio. Scamozzi visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1579 and 1580 and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1581. In 1599 and 1600 he travelled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and he left a sketchbook of his impressions of French architecture, which was first published in 1959.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Scamozzi worked mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a> and the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-palladio">Andrea Palladio</a> died in 1580 he inherited several unfinished projects and brought them to completion. They included the Villa Capra, called La Rotonda, near Vicenza, where he finished the structure and added stables, and the facade of the church of San Giorgio Maggiore in Venice, completed in 1607 to 1611. He also finished Jacopo Sansovino's Library of San Marco.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> he was invited to design the Procuratie Nuove on Piazza San Marco. It was built as housing for the Procuratorate of San Marco and presented as a unified palace front continuing the facade of Sansovino's library, with an added upper floor. He adapted a rejected project of Palladio's for the Doge's Palace. Eleven bays were completed in his time, and Baldassare Longhena, his only pupil, later extended them along the south side of the square. Scamozzi also remodelled the Teatro Olimpico in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a> in 1584 and 1585, and built the Teatro all'antica at Sabbioneta for Duke Vespasiano I Gonzaga in 1588 to 1590.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Scamozzi's influence spread through his two-volume treatise L'idea dell'architettura universale, published with woodcut illustrations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1615, one of the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> works on the theory of architecture. For parts of his treatment of Vitruvius he relied on Daniele Barbaro's commentary of 1556. He included many of his own plans and elevations, some as built, some as they should have been built, and some as idealised projects, aware that such treatises had become a means of self-promotion.</p><p>The treatise appeared a year before his death and came too late to help his own career. His practice is sometimes seen as a source of the neo-Palladian architecture introduced by Inigo Jones, and Rudolf Wittkower called him one of the intellectual fathers of neo-classicism. His earlier book on the antiquities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, published in 1583, was put together quickly, and over half its images were copied from a volume published by Hieronymus Cock in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> in 1551.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vincenzo Scamozzi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sabbioneta was laid out on a regular plan with star-shaped walls, a ducal palace, a long gallery for Vespasiano's art and antiquities, and the Teatro all'Antica by <b>Vincenzo Scamozzi</b>, completed around 1590, one of the first purpose-built theatres in modern Europe.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-sabbioneta">Duchy of Sabbioneta</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.55,11.55&amp;year=1592" aria-label="Vicenza on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4824 558 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Vincenzo Scamozzi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5274 828h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5274 828h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.55,11.55&amp;year=1592">What was here in 1592? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
